{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfrwwRWFQKTejDvYXVm38","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the off-by-one-cent totals. The full suite passes (4 tests), and the new regression test fails on the old code.\n\n**Root cause:** `invoice_total()` in `billing/totals.py` didn't add up the line amounts the PDF prints. It added up the unrounded gross and the unrounded discount amounts, then rounded once at the end. `line_totals()`, which the PDF uses, rounds each line after its discount. Whenever several discounted lines each carry a half-cent remainder, the two methods disagree by a cent. For example, two lines of 1.10 at 15% off print as 0.94 + 0.94 = 1.88, but the old code charged 1.87. Nothing raises an error, which is why the logs were clean.\n\n**Fix:** `invoice_total()` now takes `net = sum(line_totals(inv))`, so the charged amount is the sum of the printed lines. Tax is still computed on that net. The public API is unchanged.\n\n**Test:** I added `test_total_matches_sum_of_rounded_lines_with_discounts` to `tests/test_billing.py`.\n\n- **Tracked `.pyc` files:** They show as modified in `git status` because pytest rewrote them. I didn't touch them on purpose, and you may want to revert them or add them to `.gitignore`.\n- **Stash side effect:** To check the test against the old code, I stashed my change, which left a stash entry behind. I popped it after discarding the `.pyc` conflict, so my edits are all back in place.\n\nI haven't committed anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8507,"cache_read_input_tokens":52081,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8507},"output_tokens":505,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc1e9c